WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Clara Sousa-Silva: I think the public often thinks that&nbsp;
I'm spending my days just kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.534
pointing at a star and going "Oh, aliens on that&nbsp;
one! There's definitely." And it's not that.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:13.120
[Imaging Science]

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.835
[Imaging Out Of This World]

00:00:16.835 --> 00:00:19.600
[#1 Collecting Enough Light]

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.800
You know it's all about getting a really large&nbsp;
lens, effectively. We want just very large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:31.120
mirrors that are extremely carefully done and&nbsp;
very stable and very large. It becomes a kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.320
of a crude size problem and you want to focus&nbsp;
that light in and then you can process it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.760
the really expensive and really difficult bit of&nbsp;
the setup is getting that lens, that large lens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.800
And then we also have clouds. So it seems like&nbsp;
everyone here has to deal with clouds. And not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.760
just clouds, but even in a clear atmosphere, our&nbsp;
atmosphere is not visible at all wavelengths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.760
not transparent at all wavelengths. And so often&nbsp;
we need to go above the earth's atmosphere into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.280
a space telescope. But now if you want a large&nbsp;
mirror, you know the size of a tennis court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.480
which is kind of what we're aiming for, that's&nbsp;
really hard to put in space. So you end up having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:17.520
this problem of having slightly smaller lenses&nbsp;
but in space, or bigger lenses but on the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.570
And the combination of the two is really how we&nbsp;
get really good insights into alien atmospheres.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.400
[#2 Photographing Change Over Time]

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.280
Curtis Woodcock: Consistent observation is really&nbsp;
important over long periods of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:37.920
very frequently. And what you measure is&nbsp;
important, but it's more how often you measure it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.840
that really comes to light. In our work, we've&nbsp;
really only for about 10 years been able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.400
do things like grab a thousand images&nbsp;
of the same place and analyze them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.560
together to try to track the way things are&nbsp;
changing on the surface. And and one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.400
that has emerged is that the more frequently&nbsp;
you look at places the more change you find,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.160
and and that's hard to understand at first because&nbsp;
you'd like to think well, the change is either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.320
there or it isn't there, and and so if you keep&nbsp;
looking more frequently you shouldn't find more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.920
change. But you you start finding different kinds&nbsp;
of change. So there's there's kind of consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.520
seasonal patterns that you'll notice. But if you&nbsp;
look very carefully at the seasonal patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.880
they shift from year to year. And so you start,&nbsp;
if you look real frequently, you start to notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.600
the variability from one year to the next&nbsp;
year. You start to notice the variability in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.320
the climate. So you'll start to see the effect&nbsp;
of droughts, you'll start to see the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.480
particularly rainy years, you'll start to see&nbsp;
whole new different kinds of variability rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.560
than just people cutting down forests or building&nbsp;
cities or or that kind of thing. And so I've kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.160
of come to this mantra that you know, the more&nbsp;
frequently you look the more change you find and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:04.640
it was hard to understand and accept at first,&nbsp;
but that's one of the things I've learned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:11.520
the world we live in, relative to the way we&nbsp;
observe it, if you want to think of it that way.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.040
[#3 Clearing the Atmosphere]

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:21.200
It's always a fight with the atmosphere&nbsp;
of the earth and particularly my field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.360
which is trying to look for the sort of molecules&nbsp;
that could signal life. As you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.160
these are molecules we already have in this&nbsp;
atmosphere, and so it's very hard to spot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.600
them again elsewhere because the light has&nbsp;
to now go through us and then them. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.520
if you try to go further up it becomes better,&nbsp;
and that's why space telescopes are so useful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:49.040
But we do put telescopes on planes. One of the&nbsp;
telescopes that people observe is called Sofia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.480
and it's on a plane that just the side of the&nbsp;
plane opens up and there's a telescope in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.880
and you can go on the plane as an observer&nbsp;
as an astronomer and observe places far away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.560
or nearby, like you know the solar system bodies,&nbsp;
and you're on a plane which is not quite as good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.280
a space but is not quite as bad as the Earth&nbsp;
and so you end up getting a small advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.160
because you have that much less atmosphere to look&nbsp;
through. So we do put them on planes sometimes.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.840
Erika Wright: As far as the ones on Earth, you'll actually&nbsp;
know most of them are on the tops of mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.200
right? That same issue of getting through, getting&nbsp;
higher and higher into the atmosphere so you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.600
looking through less of it. Like I said, one of&nbsp;
our telescopes is in Chile it's on a mountain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:39.760
Chile. Arizona, the difference between Arizona&nbsp;
and Cambridge is quite drastic, but we do keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.200
one in Cambridge just because there is the&nbsp;
rainy season in Arizona and there are dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.800
and dozens of nights where you're just not going&nbsp;
to get any images, and thankfully in Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.720
it's not quite so bad. Then it also allows us to&nbsp;
keep one at home that we're maintaining. Like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:01.520
said it's a 25-year-old instrument, things have to&nbsp;
be kept up and maintained and fixed all the time

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:02.080
- Oiled!

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.480
- Oiled and tweaked all the time and we have&nbsp;
an engineer who does all of that regularly so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.480
that allows us to kind of have a rotating system.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:19.600
- Yes, in two weeks I'm going to go up Mauna Kea,&nbsp;
possibly the best place to see the night sky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.840
the entire Earth which is why we put quite a few&nbsp;
telescopes there, and that's exactly that, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.000
less atmosphere but it's also very dry&nbsp;
atmosphere so you want to try and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.800
as little water above you as possible, and so you&nbsp;
you're trying to really undo the damage that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.280
Earth's atmosphere does to your telescope,&nbsp;
so we are mostly very thankful for you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.040
the rain and the breathability of the planet, it&nbsp;
sometimes as an astronomer you wish you could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.960
hold your breath and not have the atmosphere&nbsp;
for a sec so we could take a better picture.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.840
[#4 What Images Communicate (and how)]

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.280
A lot of the decisions that go into that, into&nbsp;
making the images that we see as, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:07.840
receivers of NASA data, the final images,&nbsp;
are about making it something beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:14.880
and understandable so a lot of times they'll&nbsp;
choose wavelengths, they'll choose to color&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:22.400
certain wavelengths in kind of the same RGB&nbsp;
that we think about in light that we use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.520
but perhaps they'll be looking at x-ray light so&nbsp;
they'll choose three wavelengths of x-ray light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.200
and use that same red, green, and blue spectrum&nbsp;
to color it so it's a little more familiar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.960
our eyes. Sometimes you'll make a choice of&nbsp;
colorization to highlight a certain area as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.400
because it's important to the data of&nbsp;
that object. You want people to see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:50.000
perhaps a certain star, or a certain area of gas,&nbsp;
and it's more about conveying the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.480
that is known behind it even though it's not&nbsp;
that color because we can't see those colors.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.840
You know, even when we do process the images there&nbsp;
are choices that we make, there are decisions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:06.480
we make. It's not just a pure, you know, getting&nbsp;
closer to the truth process and the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.800
processes that we use to reduce this data, to&nbsp;
clean this data, try and obtain these images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:17.760
sometimes yield different results and only one of&nbsp;
them can be right, and that's a really difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.840
thing to hold in our field of work, but it's quite&nbsp;
difficult for the public to feel comfortable with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:30.320
For example, I recently worked on some high&nbsp;
profile research looking at the clouds of Venus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:38.400
to try and detect phosphine, which is here,&nbsp;
and if we process the data in a certain way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.960
phosphine shows up loud and clear but if we&nbsp;
process the date in a slightly different way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.280
not a wrong way at all, phosphine&nbsp;
disappears. And so the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.520
phosphine has to either be there or not, so&nbsp;
there is a true interpretation of these images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:00.080
and yet we don't yet know exactly what is the&nbsp;
right way of processing the data. The solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.640
you might have guessed it, is more photons, and&nbsp;
so that's what I'm trying to do up on Mauna Kea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:12.480
but even even us, you know, we're trying our&nbsp;
best and even we don't know exactly how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:17.840
process these images to get the truth out of them.&nbsp;
We don't know if we're creating a spurious signal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:22.480
when we're cleaning out noise. We don't know if&nbsp;
we clean out the noise and then clean out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:29.600
signal by mistake. And so a lot goes into these&nbsp;
processes, and I think the public often thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:36.480
that I'm spending my days just kind of pointing&nbsp;
at a star and going, "Oh, aliens on that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.480
there's definitely." And it's not that. It's&nbsp;
mostly just looking at a lot of squiggles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.800
praying that the squiggles you get rid of are&nbsp;
noise and the squiggles you keep are truths.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.760
[#5 Getting Results]

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:57.600
When when I was younger and I was starting to&nbsp;
figure out how to take, effectively, simulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:03.200
of the quantum behavior of molecules, I learned&nbsp;
all the theory, I got the supercomputer, I did all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:09.760
the right things, I solved the right equations,&nbsp;
and I did it all and then I had to show that my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:16.960
simulation, theoretical simulation, had completely&nbsp;
matched with true images of the spectrum of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:25.040
molecule. And it did! And it was only in&nbsp;
that moment when it matched that I realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.320
up until that moment I didn't believe at all that&nbsp;
my work was real, you know? It's in the quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:36.240
world, I had just clearly decided to be okay with&nbsp;
not understanding it, with it not being intuitive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:44.000
and I just didn't believe. And it was only when&nbsp;
I saw it, that it matched, that I trusted my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:50.800
work, I trusted that quantum physics is real, you&nbsp;
know. And so it was really surprising to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:57.324
out that we, as a species, have got this far where&nbsp;
we can simulate the quantum world and it works!

00:09:57.324 --> 00:10:04.420
